{"id": "cma-162453", "status": "ok", "meta": "The ten incarnations of Vishnu as seen in 1800s Bengal, a hand-colored woodcut by Shri Gobinda Chandra Roy, about 1890.", "about": "This quickly hand-colored woodcut shows ten avatars of the Hindu god Vishnu as recognized in 1800s Bengal. In each, Vishnu came down to earth to restore balance and order. The top row has the fish, tortoise, boar, man-lion and Brahmin. The bottom row has five more, from Parashurama to Kalki. Rama sits crowned with Sita, holding bow and arrow after his victory over the demon Ravana, while his monkey general Hanuman stands before him in adoration.", "museum_record": "https://clevelandart.org/art/2003.114.b", "inventory_number": "2003.114.b", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "Gift of William E. Ward in memory of his wife, Evelyn Svec Ward", "sources": ["https://clevelandart.org/art/2003.114.b", "https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/162453"], "check": "clean"}
