{"id": "si-npg_NPG.96.28", "status": "ok", "meta": "James Smithson, the chemist whose bequest founded the Smithsonian Institution, painted by James Roberts.", "about": "James Smithson, a British scientist born in Paris, laid the groundwork for the Smithsonian Institution. He had no apparent link to the United States, yet he left most of his fortune to found in Washington an establishment for the increase and diffusion of knowledge. He studied chemistry and mineralogy and published dozens of scientific papers. In 1787 he became the youngest person admitted to the Royal Society. James Roberts painted his portrait.", "museum_record": "https://npg.si.edu/object/npg_NPG.96.28", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://npg.si.edu/object/npg_NPG.96.28"], "check": "clean"}
